// Rule: T4-LOLBAS-defense-impairment-privileged-context-isolate   [STAGING]
//
// Static, single-command defence-impairment detections (MITRE T1562 and
// neighbours) — disabling/killing security tooling, blinding logging, and the
// registry downgrades that set the stage for credential theft. Modelled on the
// Huntress "Defence Impairment Olympics" i.bat: a webshell-dropped batch that
// disabled Defender, killed Sysmon/Filebeat, IFEO-neutered Sysmon/SetACL,
// downgraded WDigest, disabled IIS logging and ripped out the ModSecurity WAF
// before dumping credentials with Mimikatz.
//
// WHY T4 (MINIMAL header, NO account exclusion): every technique here runs in
// admin/SYSTEM context by design — a webshell running as the IIS worker, or a
// batch launched with SeDebug. The T1-T3 GLOBAL header excludes admin/SYSTEM,
// which would blind exactly the context these fire in. Same scope contract as
// the other two T4 files: "does not EXCLUDE privileged accounts", NOT
// "admin-only". Response tier: isolate.
//
// OVERLAP / OWNERSHIP (resolve before graduating from staging):
//   - Registry-write Defender tamper (reg add ...\Windows Defender\... , the
//     DisableAntiSpyware/DisableRealtimeMonitoring policy keys) is intentionally
//     NOT duplicated here — the existing DeviceRegistryEvents Defender-tamper
//     rule (Sigma registry_set_windows_defender_tamper) OWNS that angle over
//     DeviceRegistryEvents. These lines cover the PROCESS-COMMAND-LINE angle
//     instead (Set-MpPreference goes via WMI, WMIC exclusions, sc/taskkill),
//     which that registry rule does not see. Complementary, not overlapping.
//   - wevtutil cl (event-log clear) already lives in T3/T4 — not re-added here.
//     It IS one of the constituent techniques in the burst behaviour rule
//     (t4-...-behavior-chains), which is by design (chains reuse owned commands).
DeviceProcessEvents
// === MINIMAL EXCLUSIONS v1 — NO account exclusion (intentional) ===
| where DeviceName startswith "X"   // scope to your STAGING device prefix first
// NOTE: deliberately no InitiatingProcessAccountName exclusion here — admin/SYSTEM
// context is exactly where these techniques fire. Do not "helpfully" re-add it.
| where isnotempty(InitiatingProcessAccountName)
//| where not(InitiatingProcessFolderPath has_any ("C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Microsoft Visual Studio",
//    "C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft Visual Studio\\2022\\Professional\\"))
| where not(InitiatingProcessParentFileName == "x" and (InitiatingProcessCommandLine contains "x" and InitiatingProcessCommandLine contains "x"))
| where not(ProcessCommandLine matches regex @"x")
// === END MINIMAL EXCLUSIONS v1 ===
| where
    (1 == 2)   // inert seed — every detection below starts with `or`, so any line toggles freely
    // --- Defender tampering via command line (T1562.001) ---
    // Set-MpPreference disabling real-time/behaviour/script protection, or muting
    // cloud reporting / sample submission. The -Disable* switches are almost only
    // ever used to turn protection OFF; a re-enable (-Disable... $false) is the rare
    // FP and is left in on purpose (better to review a re-enable than miss a disable).
    // NOTE: complements the registry Defender-tamper rule — Set-MpPreference is a
    // WMI call, so it does not always surface as a Defender policy-key write.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "Set-MpPreference"
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("-DisableRealtimeMonitoring", "-DisableBehaviorMonitoring",
            "-DisableIOAVProtection", "-DisableScriptScanning", "-DisableBlockAtFirstSeen",
            "-DisableIntrusionPreventionSystem", "-DisableOnAccessProtection",
            "-MAPSReporting Disabled", "-MAPSReporting 0",
            "-SubmitSamplesConsent NeverSend", "-SubmitSamplesConsent 2"))
    // WMIC creating a Defender exclusion path/extension/process — the classic
    // "carve out a safe folder for my tools" step (T1562.001). Near-zero benign.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "MpPreference" and ProcessCommandLine has "Add"
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("ExclusionPath", "ExclusionExtension", "ExclusionProcess"))
    // --- Killing / stopping security + logging tooling (T1562.001) ---
    // taskkill targeting named EDR/AV/logging processes. Scoped to product binaries
    // so it fires on "kill my defences", not on routine process management.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "taskkill.exe"
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("sysmon.exe", "sysmon64.exe", "filebeat.exe",
            "cyserver.exe", "McPanel.Log.exe", "SentinelMemoryScanner.exe", "SentinelUI.exe",
            "SentinelAgent.exe", "SentinelHelperService.exe", "DRwebcom.exe", "MsSense.exe",
            "MsMpEng.exe", "SenseIR.exe", "elastic-agent.exe", "winlogbeat.exe"))
    // sc.exe stop/delete/config targeting named security/logging services. Same
    // scoping rationale: stopping/deleting *these* services is the impairment,
    // whereas generic sc create/config lives in T2.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "sc.exe" and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("stop", "delete", "config")
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("sysmon", "sysmondrv", "filebeat", "WinDefend",
            "Sense", "cyserver", "LogProcessorService", "ProvisioningClient",
            "SentinelStaticEngine", "SentinelAgent", "SentinelHelperService",
            "mr2kserv", "elastic-agent", "MDCoreSvc"))
    // WMIC stopping a security service by name pattern (e.g. Immunet/ClamAV family).
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "wmic" and ProcessCommandLine has "service"
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("stopService", "call stop")
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("Immunet", "Sentinel", "Sysmon", "Defender", "clamav"))
    // net stop of a named security service — the net.exe/net1.exe sibling of sc stop.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has_any ("net.exe", "net1.exe") and ProcessCommandLine has "stop"
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("Sense", "WinDefend", "Sysmon", "Sentinel", "filebeat"))
    // --- Blinding logging / neutering binaries (T1562.002 / T1546.012) ---
    // Image File Execution Options "Debugger" hijack: pointing a binary at a stub
    // debugger (ntsd -d, "cmd /c", etc.) freezes/redirects it on launch. Attackers
    // use it to neuter Sysmon/Filebeat/SetACL; legitimate IFEO Debugger use is rare.
    // Tool-agnostic: keys on the IFEO key + Debugger value, not the writing binary.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "Image File Execution Options" and ProcessCommandLine has "Debugger"
        and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("add", "/v"))
    // IIS HTTP logging turned off via appcmd — blinds the very logs used to find
    // other webshells POSTed to the server (T1562.002).
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "appcmd" and ProcessCommandLine has "httpLogging"
        and ProcessCommandLine has "dontLog")
    // Uninstalling an IIS module via appcmd — used here to remove the ModSecurity
    // WAF, stripping SQLi/XSS protection off the server. Admins rarely uninstall
    // IIS modules this way on a live server.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "appcmd" and ProcessCommandLine has_all ("uninstall", "module"))
    // --- Credential-protection downgrade (T1112 -> T1003.001 setup) ---
    // WDigest UseLogonCredential = 1 forces plaintext creds back into LSASS memory,
    // the classic pre-Mimikatz step. The secure default is 0, so require the enable
    // and exclude the hardening direction (/d 0) to avoid flagging remediation.
    or (ProcessCommandLine has "UseLogonCredential" and ProcessCommandLine has "WDigest"
        and not(ProcessCommandLine has "/d 0"))
| extend Severity = "Critical"
| project Timestamp, Severity, DeviceName, AccountName, ProcessCommandLine, FileName,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessParentFileName, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by Timestamp desc

// --- OVERLAP NOTES (resolve before graduating from staging) ---
// 1. This is a NEW class (T1562 defence impairment) alongside the existing T4
//    cred-access/impact/lateral file. No single command here is owned by another
//    tier today, so there is no static double-fire to dedupe — EXCEPT wevtutil cl,
//    which is intentionally NOT included here (owned by T3/T4) and only reappears
//    inside the burst behaviour rule as a constituent technique.
// 2. Registry-write Defender tamper stays with the DeviceRegistryEvents rule; the
//    Set-MpPreference / WMIC-exclusion lines here are the process-visible siblings
//    that rule cannot see. If you later want a single Defender-tamper incident
//    owner, route the registry rule as owner and these as enrichment (or vice
//    versa) — same isolate action either way, so it is attribution, not conflict.
// 3. FP watch during staging: Set-MpPreference re-enables (-Disable... $false) and
//    legitimate AV-migration windows (third-party AV install disabling Defender).
//    Tune by InitiatingProcessCommandLine/parent once your baseline is known.
